Register-resident attention from MLX

Upstream revision: 25532871329fe4cafdada9fa0941a1cdbfb3a9f2
Repository: https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx
License: MIT (included in attention.metal.inc).

This file embeds the following upstream headers in include order, with duplicate
includes and pragma-once directives removed:
  mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/attn/kernels/steel_attention_nax.h
  mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/attn/nax.h
  mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/defines.h
  mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/utils/integral_constant.h
  mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/utils/type_traits.h
  mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/attn/params.h
  mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/attn/transforms.h
  mlx/backend/metal/kernels/steel/utils.h

Local changes:
- Replace Limits<T>::finite_min with -metal::numeric_limits<T>::max() so the
  unrelated general MLX type/utilities headers are unnecessary.
- Store saved softmax statistics as log2 half values to match MFA backward.
- Wrap the source in a C++ raw string. Kernel instantiations and function
  constants live in NARegisterAttentionKernel.cpp.

The integration currently selects FP16, unmasked, noncausal D=128 (R>=256)
or D=256 (R>64) forward attention. D256 causal inference also uses this kernel
when R<=C, with qL_off=C-R for a growing KV cache. Causal masking is a pipeline
constant; training keeps its existing causal path. SHD strides are passed
directly; no tensor layout conversion occurs.

Register-fragment GEMM

gemm.metal.inc embeds the same upstream revision's utils.h, steel/gemm/gemm_nax.h
and steel/gemm/kernels/steel_gemm_fused_nax.h with their transitive local includes.
Duplicate includes and pragma-once directives are removed, and the result is
wrapped in a C++ raw string. Its embedded MIT license and upstream copyright
notices are retained. No Metal kernel arithmetic is changed.

NARegisterMatMulKernel instantiates FP16 NT 64x128x512 and 128x64x512 profiles
with eight SIMD groups and FP32 accumulation. Bias is broadcast through the
upstream addmm epilogue. Dimensions and strides are passed at encoding time.
The 128x64 profile and X/Y swizzle are used when N > M, matching MLX's Ultra
orientation. Selection uses the shared Apple10 capability predicate; no model-name gate remains.

The FP16 selector requires B=1, contiguous NT input/output, N>=1536.
It accepts M>=4096, explicit FP32 accumulation at M>=512, or half-allowed
accumulation at M>=2048 with at least 4096*1536 output elements. Compact
full-tile moderate reductions retain the native MPP profile.
Unsplit traversal covers 2048<=K<=8192, or K<=16384 with K<3*N, FP16 weights
no larger than 192 MiB, and either M<N or M>=2*N. These are measured performance bounds. The previous
implementation remains available for narrow/small outputs and larger reductions.

gemm-splitk.metal.inc embeds steel/gemm/kernels/steel_gemm_splitk_nax.h from the
same upstream revision. It removes the duplicate align_M/align_N declarations,
already supplied by gemm.metal.inc; otherwise the upstream kernel is unchanged.
The local reduction sums FP32 partials and adds an optional broadcast FP16 bias.
A partition-major 1D grid uses 4096-element K partitions. Selection additionally
requires M<N, K>=2*N, K<=32768, and at most 256 MiB per FP32 partial output. This
path takes precedence over unsplit traversal. The public scratch reservation
conservatively covers both old and new partial outputs to protect decoded weights.

Tile orientation and split/unsplit traversal are source-generation cache
properties. Alignment/bias are pipeline constants; dimensions, partition count,
strides, swizzle and grid size remain runtime values. The latest measurements
and boundary controls are documented in inference-fp16-medium.txt and gating-review.txt in
doc/benchmarks/m5-ultra-na-2026-10-02/.

NARegisterMatMulKernel also supplies a local INT8 entry point using the same
unchanged fragment loads, 16x32x16 register multiply, and gemm_loop traversal.
It accumulates INT8 products in INT32 and applies MFA's existing activation and
weight row scales, plus optional bias, before the FP16 store. The upstream
gemm.metal.inc is unchanged; the INT8 epilogue is in the local C++ generator.
The integer/FP16 choice is a source-generation cache property. Alignment and
bias remain pipeline constants; matrix sizes and dispatch grids remain runtime
values. No additional hardware-name helper is introduced.

The INT8 generator unrolls pairs of K32 fragment operations and uses ordinary
loads for complete K32 fragments inside a partial outer K512 tile. The final
fragment retains bounds checks. Its optional Float32 store widens an already
rounded half result, preserving the GEMM-then-cast contract. Both changes are
local to NARegisterMatMulKernel.cpp, not edits to the embedded upstream source.
